      "target_id": "ckl:mesopotamian-creation-and-flood-comparisons",
      "textual_observation": "Genesis 1:1-2:3 narrates cosmic ordering through divine speech, separation, naming, and appointment. Enuma Elish narrates divine conflict culminating in Marduk’s ordering of the cosmos and supremacy. Genesis 1:2 uses Hebrew tĕhôm (“the deep”); comparisons with Tiamat have a history in scholarship, but vocabulary alone does not establish a literary link, and Genesis does not narrate Marduk’s combat or divine succession.",
      "uncertainty": "The texts share creation imagery, but this comparison alone does not establish direct literary dependence or a single direction of influence.",
      "why": "The connection presents Genesis alongside Mesopotamian creation traditions as comparative context while keeping their distinct settings visible."
