      "target_id": "ckl:the-flood",
      "textual_observation": "Genesis 6:9-9:17 and Gilgamesh Tablet XI share a recognizable cluster of flood-story motifs: warning, a vessel, survival of a household and living things, birds released after the waters, landing, and post-flood offering. Their divine portrayals and narrative aims differ; the CKL treats the tablet as comparative literature, not an archaeological identification of Noah’s flood.",
      "uncertainty": "The shared motif cluster is observable, but it does not by itself establish direct dependence or a single transmission route. Tablet K.3375 is a seventh-century BCE copy of an older tradition; its copy date does not date that tradition or settle the composition history of Genesis. The relationship between Genesis 6-9 and Mesopotamian flood traditions remains debated.",
      "why": "This route lets readers compare a biblical flood narrative with a specific Mesopotamian witness while keeping motif comparison separate from proof of dependence or event identity."
